The code statements that lead to the bug are:
len = ALIGN(le32_to_cpu(p->ErrorDataLength), 8);
p = (struct smb2_error_context_rsp *)(p->ErrorContextData + len);
Reason:
On 32-bit architectures, when `p->ErrorDataLength` is a large value (e.g., 0xfffffff8), `ALIGN(..., 8)` can produce a value that, when added to the pointer `p->ErrorContextData`, causes pointer arithmetic to wrap around. This can either leave `p` unchanged (infinite loop) or move it to a memory location before the buffer (out-of-bounds read), because no bounds check verifies that `len` fits within the remaining buffer space.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
if (len > end - ((u8 *)p + sizeof(*p)))
    return ERR_PTR(-EINVAL);
Reason:
This check ensures that the computed `len` does not exceed the number of bytes remaining in the buffer after the current error context header. If it does, the function returns an error early, preventing the pointer arithmetic from overflowing and thus eliminating both the infinite loop and the out-of-bounds read.

